 ## Overview
-The Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture is a hardware design methodology for volume rendering that achieves real-time performance by time-slicing functional blocks to allow the simultaneous processing of multiple tasks [S1, unofficial]. It solves the latency and throughput bottlenecks of sequential rendering by overlapping execution stages, enabling continuous communication between hardware components and software control [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard pipelined approaches by explicitly reusing embedded sub-cores across different pipeline stages via a shared context memory, rather than dedicating fixed hardware to a single stage [S1, unofficial].
+The Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture is a hardware design methodology for volume rendering systems that achieves real-time performance by time-slicing functional blocks to allow the simultaneous processing of multiple tasks [S1, unofficial]. It solves the bottleneck of sequential hardware execution by overlapping pipeline stages, enabling continuous communication between hardware components and software interfaces [S1, unofficial]. Unlike general-purpose DSP-based solutions that require multiple chips for similar volumes, this architecture integrates the entire rendering pipeline into a single system using reusable sub-cores, allowing for efficient layout and configuration based on specific processing requirements [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The architecture implements the shear-warp factorization volume rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial].
+The architecture decomposes the volume rendering pipeline into independent, reusable functional blocks (sub-cores) that are time-sliced to execute concurrently.
 
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** Volume data, transformation matrices, classification parameters.
-*   **Output:** Rendered image pixels.
